 See also [how to shard data among multiple remote storage systems](#sharding-among-remote-storages).
 
+## Limiting the number of targets per job
+
+By default, `vmagent` doesn't limit the number of active scrape targets a single scrape job (identified by `job_name`)
+can have, regardless of which [service discovery](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/) mechanism(s)
+supplied the targets. This means that a misconfiguration on the discovery side (for example, an accidentally removed
+tag filter or an overly broad catalog query) can suddenly return a huge number of targets for a single job. Scraping all
+of them at once can spike CPU and memory usage on the `vmagent` instance and degrade scraping for every other job running
+on it.
+
+To protect against this, the maximum number of simultaneously scraped targets per `job_name` can be limited in the following places:
+
+* Via `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag. This limit is applied individually to every `job_name`
+  defined in the file pointed to by `-promscrape.config`.
+* Via `max_scrape_targets_per_job` config option at [scrape_config](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs) section.
+  The `max_scrape_targets_per_job` allows overriding the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` on a per-`scrape_config` basis.
+
+By default (both the flag and the per-job option are unset, i.e. `0`), the number of targets per job is unlimited, so
+existing setups aren't affected.
+
+When a job's discovered target count exceeds the configured limit, `vmagent` keeps scraping only up to that many targets
+for the job and drops the rest &mdash; it doesn't crash, reject the whole job, or silently scrape everything. The limit
+is enforced uniformly no matter which `*_sd_configs` (or `static_configs`) produced the targets for the job.
+
+Targets excluded because of this limit are shown as `DROPPED (job targets limit)` at the `http://vmagent:8429/targets`
+and `http://vmagent:8429/service-discovery` pages, alongside targets dropped for other reasons (e.g. relabeling), so it is
+easy to notice which job(s) are currently over the limit and how many targets are being excluded because of it. The same
+information is available in machine-readable form at the `droppedTargets` array returned by `http://vmagent:8429/api/v1/targets`.
+
 ## High availability
 
 It is possible to run multiple **identically configured** `vmagent` instances or `vmagent`
